Activity 1 — Encapsulation Review
| Class        | Protected State / Invariant                           | Public Operations                          |
|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|
| Patient      | patientID never changes; name/contact must be valid   | getDetails(), updateContact()               |
| Practitioner | availability must follow clinic rules; ID fixed        | getSchedule(), updateAvailability()         |
| Appointment  | status must follow legal transitions; time fixed       | cancel(), complete(), reschedule()          |

Activity 2 — Composition or Inheritance:
Appointment - Patient
Composition/association
Reason: An appointment has a patient. A patient is not a type of appointment. This is a relationship, not inheritance.

Appointment - Practitioner
Composition/association
Reason: An appointment has a practitioner. A practitioner is not a subtype of appointment.

Doctor - Practitioner (hypothetical)
Inheritance
Reason: A doctor is a type of practitioner with extra attributes. 

Clinic - Appointment
Composition/association
Reason: A clinic contains appointments. The clinic is an aggregate root, not a superclass.

Activity 3 — Responsibility Allocation:
Who decides whether SCHEDULED can become CANCELLED?
 - Appointment class: Status transitions are part of the appointment’s internal rules (invariant).
   The UI must not decide business rules.

Who validates a patient name?
 - Patient class: Validation belongs inside the domain model, not the UI.

Should Appointment execute SQL? Why?
No.  
SQL belongs in the data access layer. Domain objects should not know how data is stored — keeps them pure and testable.

Should the UI decide whether a status transition is legal?
No.  
UI only triggers actions. The domain model enforces rules.

Activity 4 — AI Code Critique
AI generated an Appointment class with:
- public status mutation
- SQL inside cancel()
- NotificationManager dependency
- inheritance from PatientRecord
- other over-designed elements

Five design problems + corrections
Public status mutation
Problem: Anyone can change status → breaks invariants.
Fix: Make status private; expose controlled methods like cancel() or complete().

SQL inside cancel()
Problem: Violates separation of concerns; domain object shouldn’t talk to the database.
Fix: Move SQL to a repository class.

NotificationManager dependency
Problem: Appointment shouldn’t know about external services.
Fix: Use an event or service layer to trigger notifications.

Inheritance from PatientRecord
Problem: Appointment is not a type of patient record → incorrect hierarchy.
Fix: Remove inheritance; use composition (appointment has a patient).

Too many responsibilities
Problem: Appointment is doing scheduling, notifications, persistence.
Fix: Apply Single Responsibility Principle — keep Appointment focused on domain rules only.

Exit Question — Why can code be object‑oriented syntactically but still have poor OO design?
ecause syntax ≠ design.
A program can use classes, objects, inheritance, and still be poorly designed if:
- responsibilities are mixed
- domain rules live in the UI
- classes expose internal state
- inheritance is misused
- objects depend on infrastructure (SQL, APIs)
- invariants are not protected
Good OO design is about responsibility, boundaries, and behaviour, not just using the class keyword.